{"schema_version":"1.9.2","id":"CLSA-2026-1793979856","published":"2026-11-06T15:44:36Z","modified":"2026-11-20T10:17:13.320430916Z","upstream":["CVE-2025-8714","CVE-2026-18408"],"summary":"Fix of 17 CVEs","details":"   * SECURITY UPDATE: psql meta-commands injected through plain-text dumps.\n     pg_dump, pg_dumpall and pg_restore copy object definitions and table\n     data from the source server into their plain-text scripts verbatim,\n     so a malicious or compromised source server could place psql\n     meta-commands such as \\! in the dump, and psql ran them on the\n     machine restoring the dump, as the user running psql.\n     - debian/patches/CVE-2025-8714.patch: add a \"restricted mode\" to psql\n       (\\restrict KEY refuses every meta-command until \\unrestrict KEY with\n       the same key) and make pg_dump, pg_dumpall and pg_restore wrap every\n       plain-text script they write, by pg_restore both to --file and to\n       standard output, in \\restrict/\\unrestrict with a random key, settable\n       with the new --restrict-key option.  Where the script switches\n       databases inside the restricted section (pg_dump and pg_restore\n       --create, and pg_dumpall moving the postgres or template1 database to\n       another tablespace), the \\connect line is preceded by \\unrestrict and\n       followed by \\restrict, so psql runs the \\connect itself unrestricted\n       and a restore with ON_ERROR_STOP does not stop at it; pg_dumpall\n       writes the \\connect for each database it dumps between the restricted\n       sections (upstream c204bd39f7, ae63671a7f, e8bc40d290).  PostgreSQL\n       9.5 has no pg_strong_random(), so the key is read from /dev/urandom\n       and the dump fails if that is not possible.\n     - debian/patches/CVE-2026-18408.patch: read the \\unrestrict argument\n       up to the end of the line without backquote or variable expansion;\n       otherwise a dump could still run shell commands through\n       \"\\unrestrict `command`\" while restricted (upstream 2006fca401).\n     - BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: every plain-text dump now starts with\n       \"\\restrict <key>\" and ends with \"\\unrestrict <key>\", so two dumps of\n       the same database are no longer byte-identical unless the same\n       --restrict-key is given, and a psql without this update prints\n       \"invalid command \\restrict\" when restoring such a dump (with\n       ON_ERROR_STOP it stops there, before restoring anything; otherwise\n       the restore continues unprotected).  Scripts that insert their own\n       meta-commands into a dump must place them outside the\n       \\restrict/\\unrestrict section or use a known --restrict-key.\n       Custom, directory and tar archives are unchanged.\n     - CVE-2025-8714\n     - CVE-2026-18408","affected":[{"package":{"name":"libecpg-compat3","ecosystem":"TuxCare:Ubuntu:16.04","purl":"pkg:deb/tuxcare/libecpg-compat3?distro=ubuntu-16.04"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"0"},{"fixed":"9.5.25-0ubuntu0.16.04.1+tuxcare.els22"}]}],"database_specific":{"source":"https://github.com/cloudlinux/tuxcare-osv/tree/main/data/els_os/ubuntu16.04els/CLSA-2026-1793979856.json"}},{"package":{"name":"libecpg-dev","ecosystem":"TuxCare:Ubuntu:16.04","purl":"pkg:deb/tuxcare/libecpg-dev?distro=ubuntu-16.04"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"0"},{"fixed":"9.5.25-0ubuntu0.16.04.1+tuxcare.els22"}]}],"database_specific":{"source":"https://github.com/cloudlinux/tuxcare-osv/tree/main/data/els_os/ubuntu16.04els/CLSA-2026-1793979856.json"}},{"package":{"name":"libecpg6","ecosystem":"TuxCare:Ubuntu:16.04","purl":"pkg:deb/tuxcare/libecpg6?distro=ubuntu-16.04"},"ranges":[{"type":"ECOSYSTEM","events":[{"introduced":"0"},{"fixed":"9.5.25-0ubuntu0.16.04.1+tuxcare.els22"}]}],"database_specific":{"source":"https://github.com/cloudlinux/tuxcare-osv/tree/main/data/els_os/ubuntu16.04els/CLSA-2026-1793979856.json"}},{"package":{"name":"libpgtypes3","ecosystem":"TuxCare: